<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">College of William &amp; Mary</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1693">1693</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as College of William and Mary</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The College of William &amp; Mary is a public university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/williamsburg-virginia">Williamsburg, Virginia</a>, founded by royal charter in 1693 under King William III and Queen Mary II. It is the second-oldest institution of higher education in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, after Harvard. Its students included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-monroe">James Monroe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-marshall">John Marshall</a>, and the Phi Beta Kappa society was founded there in 1776.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>A school of higher education for Native American young men and the sons of colonists was among the earliest aims of the leaders of the Colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>. Plans dating from 1618 at Henrico were thwarted by the attack of 1622, the end of the Virginia Company's charter in 1624, events connected with the English Civil War and Bacon's Rebellion.</p><p>The royal charter was granted on 8 February 1693 under King William III and Queen Mary II, to found a place of universal study and a perpetual college of divinity, philosophy, languages and other arts and sciences. Construction of the College Building, now called the Sir Christopher Wren Building, began in 1695. The charter named James Blair first president, a post he held until his death in 1743. The college was founded as an Anglican institution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>C. Society, popularly the Flat Hat Club, in 1750 and Phi Beta Kappa in 1776. In 1779, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> and with reformers including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>, the college abolished its Divinity School and Indian School, established what is described as the first American university-affiliated law school and introduced the first elective system of study and an honour system.</p><p>At the outset of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> enlistments depleted the student body, and on 10 May 1861 the faculty voted to close the college for the duration. The College Building served as a Confederate barracks and then as a hospital, and in September 1862 drunken soldiers of the 5th Pennsylvania Cavalry set it on fire.</p><p>Benjamin Stoddert Ewell reopened the college in 1869 with his own funds, and it closed again in 1882 for lack of funding. It resumed in 1888 as a teacher-training institution after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> appropriated $10,000. In 1906 the Virginia General Assembly took over the grounds, and the college has been publicly supported since. In 1918 it was one of the first universities in Virginia to admit women. In 1926 the college's W. A. R. Goodwin persuaded John D. Rockefeller Jr. to begin the restoration of Williamsburg, which led to Colonial Williamsburg, and the Wren Building was restored in 1928–1931.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>William &amp; Mary is a public university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/williamsburg-virginia">Williamsburg, Virginia</a>, on a campus of 1,200 acres bordering the colonial town. Its alumni include the presidents <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-monroe">James Monroe</a> and John Tyler, Chief Justice John Marshall and Speaker Henry Clay. George Washington received his surveyor's license from the college in 1749 and later served as its first American chancellor. The Wren Building, designated a National Historic Landmark, is described as the oldest college building in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “College of William &amp; Mary” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Church of England was established, and the <b>College of William and Mary</b> was founded in 1693.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-virginia">Colony of Virginia</a></li></ul></section>
